  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nCURRENT & TONIGHT: A bright and mild day continues! Taking a look \nacross the area, mostly clear conditions persist across the area \nwith some marginal breeziness. With some weak ridging approaching \naloft, could see a temporary decrease in wind speed this afternoon \nand evening before an uptick in speeds occurs overnight as the SPG \ntightens. With a surface warm front on schedule to lift through the \narea overnight, should see clouds begin to build in as mid-level \nwarm air advection (WAA) strengthens aloft. Given the conditions, \ndecided to bump up overnight lows slightly by blending in some of \nthe CONSshort. This puts most areas in the upper 30s to mid 40s for \nthe night.\n\nTHE WEEKEND: Looking into the weekend, a few spotty showers could \ndevelop north of I-90 by Friday morning as the warm front continues \nto lift northwards. However, any accumulations should be limited. \nConfidence is high that the drearier conditions will persist through \nat least the first half of day. From here, guidance is still a bit \nsplit on if diurnal heating scowers out our stratus or not. This \nwill play a pivotal role in how strong our winds become for tomorrow \nas thicker stratus could hinder mixing and thus keeping the \nstrongest winds aloft. Either way, southerly winds are expected to \nstrengthen ahead of an approaching cold front as the SPG continues \nto tight leading to gusts between 35-45 mph at times. While some \nareas could briefly touch wind advisory criteria, current thinking \nis lingering stratus will disrupt things just enough to keep any \nadvisory level gusts more spotty vs widespread. With this in mind, \ndecided to hold off on any wind headline decisions at this time. \n\nFrom here, there's some low to moderate potential (30%-40%) for a \nfew light showers to develop as the surface cold front progresses \nthrough the region. Any accumulations would be light. By Saturday, \nwe'll begin to trend colder as a strong push of cold air advection \n(CAA) progresses through the region with the 850mb cold front. This \nalong with northwesterly surface winds will help highs gradually \ndecrease from the low to mid 60s on Friday to the mid 40s to mid 50s \nSaturday then the mid 30s to low 40s by Sunday. Otherwise, can't \ncompletely rule out a few light shower in our far southeastern zones \n(Buena Vista, Cherokee, and Ida counties in northwestern IA) by late \nSaturday. However, any accumulations will be light given the limited \nsaturation in soundings. Lastly, Sunday looks to be a much quieter \nday as a surface high returns with quasi-zonal flow aloft.\n\nNext Week: Heading into next week, the quiet and cool conditions \nwill persist through at least Tuesday as ridging builds. Highs \nmainly in the low to mid 30s will keep things pretty chilly so make \nsure to dress appropriately during this time as temperatures could \nstart in the teens to low 20s each morning! Looking aloft, ridging \nwill likely be replaced by a quick mid-level wave by Wednesday \nmorning. While confidence is low (30% or less), its worth mentioning \nthat some medium to long-range guidance is starting to show some \nlight snow moving into areas along and west of the James River \nValley. This is further agreed upon in the ensemble guidance with \nthe grand ensemble showing low to moderate confidence (20%-40%) in a \ndusting or less. Lastly, the return of a southerly component to the \nsurface winds by Wednesday could help temperatures gradually recover \ninto the late week with highs possibly reaching the mid 30s to low \n40s from Wednesday into Thursday.",
